Most code is from the MPlayer project.
- Bastiaan Stougie <wififreedom2026@protonmail.com>
  Sep. 2026:
  - option --replacements FILE
    Specify a file with regular expression match patterns and replacement
    patterns. The replacements will be applied immediately after OCR, before
    optional italic detection. A sample replacements file is available in the
    source code repository: replacements/replacements_EN.txt
  - option --detect-italic:
    Detect text that is italic and mark it as such in the output .srt file with
    <i> and </i> tags. Uses OpenCV contour detection to improve bounding boxes
    received from Tesseract, then looks at the leftmost symbol pixel in the top
    row and bottom row of the symbol bounding box to determine, based on
    collected statistics, if a symbol is italic or not. Propagates the results
    to the word and neighbouring words.
  - option --ocr-batch-size:
    Combine multiple subtitle images into one image for increased OCR accuracy
    and to prevent that OCR does not find any text if there's only a little
    text in a subtitle image.
  - options --base-duration and --chars-per-sec:
    Limit maximum subtitle display information based on a base duration plus
    a duration based on the minimum number of characters per second a viewer is
    to read. This prevents that subtitles linger on screen until the next
    subtitle appears, and provides the reader with enough time to read them.
  - options --debug-number and --debug-images:
    for debugging
  - fix for broken mplayer code that read past the end of a buffer:
    This could also cause an invalid palette and therefore broken images, with
    a bad OCR result.
    See also https://trac.mplayerhq.hu/ticket/2432 .
  - fix for skipped subtitle images:
    No longer process packet fragments and look at start_pts to figure out if a
    new packet has arrived. Instead, wait for a packet to have been completely
    assembled from its fragments, then process it.
    The old way of looking at fragments and start_pts skips subtitle images if
    start_pts is the same for multiple packets.
  - automatic threshold limit detection:
    Based on image color histogram. Better OCR results than with hard-coded
    threshold value 128, which can lose too much information.
  - ability to dump subtitle images in any file format OpenCV supports
    Library libpng no longer required, OpenCV used instead.
  - exception safety and exception handling
  - always replace "|" received from Tesseract with "I".
